## 6. SRAM on the Boards Without a Panel

The heap is whatever the kernel image leaves of the RP2350's 520 KB, and a Pico 2 W's boot, Wi-Fi and a compile (the compiler takes a 112 KB arena at once) use most of it. Three build settings keep it as large as the board allows (/proc/meminfo, Pages Total):

```
| Setting | Where | What it gives back |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| One reader window | READERMAXINSTANCES in drivers/include/drivers/readerview.h: 1 on an RP2350 without CONFIGLCDPCLKGPIO (and on the C6), 4 elsewhere | 22 KB |
| No core-1 stack | _stack1size in CMakeLists.txt: 16 KB only where the SMP scheduler or the PIO-USB engine (CONFIGPIOUSBDP_GPIO, the terminal) runs on core 1, none elsewhere | 16 KB |
| A sensor skip-list | CONFIGSENSORSKIP in the board file, comma-separated driver names the hub never registers. The Pico 2 W skips mics6814: with nothing wired, its probe of 0x04 was answered at boot by nothing, and the hub then ran a 12 KB sampler task for the rest of the uptime | 12 KB of task stack on the boots it triggered |
```

With them a Pico 2 W has 80 heap pages (the build before the update layer had 71). Lisp's pools are the next candidates, and are board settings already (CONFIGLISPNODEPOOL, CONFIGLISPSTRINGPOOL; the C6 runs 512 nodes and 192 strings, the RP2350 default is 1024 nodes and about 26 KB of strings), but they decide how much Lisp a board can run, and are left alone until a board needs it.

